applyconfiguration-gen: With<Field> sets the shadowed promoted field when a direct field has the same name, so the value is lost

A struct embeds a struct inline, and also declares a direct field with the same Go name and JSON name as a promoted field. The generated `With<Field>` setter writes to the promoted field. `encoding/json` serializes only the direct (less nested) field, so the value is silently lost:

```
type Base struct {
	Enabled bool   `json:"enabled,omitempty"`
	Mode    string `json:"mode,omitempty"`
}

type Override struct {
	Base    `json:",inline"`
	Enabled *bool `json:"enabled,omitempty"`
}
```

Generated:

```
func (b *OverrideApplyConfiguration) WithEnabled(value bool) *OverrideApplyConfiguration {
	b.BaseApplyConfiguration.Enabled = &value
	return b
}
```

```
json.Marshal(v1.Override{Enabled: &t})        -> {"enabled":true}
json.Marshal(ac.Override().WithEnabled(true)) -> {}
```

The code compiles. The apply request does not contain the field.

`WithEnabled` sets the direct field `b.Enabled`, which shadows the promoted field in Go and in JSON.

Use the two types above in a `+genclient` type, as in this example:

```
type WidgetSpec struct {
	Override Override `json:"override,omitempty"`
}

// +genclient
type Widget struct {
	metav1.TypeMeta   `json:",inline"`
	metav1.ObjectMeta `json:"metadata,omitempty"`
	Spec              WidgetSpec `json:"spec,omitempty"`
}
```

```
go run k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/applyconfiguration-gen@v0.37.1 \
  --output-dir ./ac --output-pkg example.com/repro/ac ./api/v1
```

`generateWithFuncs` in `applyconfiguration.go` goes through the members in declaration order. It skips a setter whose member name it already generated. When the embedded struct comes first, the promoted setter takes the name, and the setter for the direct field is not generated. If the direct field comes first, the output is correct. A possible fix: generate the setters for the direct fields before the setters of embedded structs. This follows the Go rule that the less nested field wins.

applyconfiguration-gen: apply configurations ignore custom MarshalJSON/UnmarshalJSON of a struct type

A struct type with JSON-tagged fields and custom `MarshalJSON`/`UnmarshalJSON` methods gets a field-by-field apply configuration. The apply configuration does not have the custom methods. As a result, the apply request has a different wire format than the typed object:

```
typed object:        {"address":"a:1"}
apply configuration: {"address":{"host":"a","port":"1"}}
```

The API server sees a different value, or rejects the request. The generated code compiles, so nothing reports the problem.

A type with custom JSON serialization is an atomic value. The generator uses the Go type in the parent apply configuration (`WithAddress(value v1.Address)`) and generates no field-by-field builder. This is the same as for types that have no JSON-tagged members, for example `resource.Quantity`.

`api/v1/types.go` (with `// +groupName=example.com` in `doc.go`):

```
package v1

import (
	"encoding/json"

	metav1 "k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/apis/meta/v1"
)

type Address struct {
	Host string `json:"host,omitempty"`
[... 24 more lines]
```

```
go run k8s.io/code-generator/cmd/applyconfiguration-gen@v0.37.1 \
  --output-dir ./ac --output-pkg example.com/repro/ac ./api/v1
```

```
typed, _ := json.Marshal(v1.WidgetSpec{Address: v1.Address{Host: "a", Port: "1"}})
apply, _ := json.Marshal(ac.WidgetSpec().WithAddress(ac.Address().WithHost("a").WithPort("1")))
// typed: {"address":"a:1"}
// apply: {"address":{"host":"a","port":"1"}}
```

`requiresApplyConfiguration` in `refgraph.go` looks only at JSON-tagged members. A possible fix: also return false when the type, or a pointer to it, has a `MarshalJSON` or `UnmarshalJSON` method. The parent then uses the type as a whole value.

Make `conversion-gen` automatically pick up autogenerated conversion functions of types across packages

Right now, if you have a cross-package type reference, e.g. the `authorization.k8s.io` type `AuthorizationConditionsRequest` refers to `AdmissionRequest` in `admission.k8s.io`, `conversion-gen` fails with

```
	if in.AdmissionRequest != nil {
		in, out := &in.AdmissionRequest, &out.AdmissionRequest
		*out = new(admission.AdmissionRequest)
		// FIXME: Provide conversion function to convert admissionv1.AdmissionRequest to admission.AdmissionRequest
		compileErrorOnMissingConversion()
	} else {
		out.AdmissionRequest = nil
	}
```

in the generated code on the `authorization.k8s.io` side. This is because `conversion-gen` doesn't "see" any ready-made conversion for `AdmissionRequest` between its internal and external versions. However, `conversion-gen` is in fact responsible for generating that conversion too, hence it doesn't exist up front.

This can be worked around by adding the following to `pkg/apis/admission/v1/conversion.go`:

```
// Convert_v1_AdmissionRequest_To_admission_AdmissionRequest just calls the covering autogenerated function, but is defined and thus
// "exported" in conversion.go, such that it is usable by other API groups that embed AdmissionRequest.
func Convert_v1_AdmissionRequest_To_admission_AdmissionRequest(in *admissionv1.AdmissionRequest, out *admission.AdmissionRequest, s conversion.Scope) error {
	return autoConvert_v1_AdmissionRequest_To_admission_AdmissionRequest(in, out, s)
}

// Convert_admission_AdmissionRequest_To_v1_AdmissionRequest just calls the covering autogenerated function, but is defined and thus
// "exported" in conversion.go, such that it is usable by other API groups that embed AdmissionRequest.
func Convert_admission_AdmissionRequest_To_v1_AdmissionRequest(in *admission.AdmissionRequest, out *admissionv1.AdmissionRequest, s conversion.Scope) error {
	return autoConvert_admission_AdmissionRequest_To_v1_AdmissionRequest(in, out, s)
[... 1 more lines]
```

I want `conversion-gen` to understand what `Convert_X_Z_To_Y_Z` functions it will generate, and call those automatically when creating the conversions.

Consider the type structure e.g. in https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/pull/137633
Make a cross-API-package type reference in core Kubernetes, and try to generate a conversion.

validateQualifiedName / resourcesQualifiedName silently accept names with more than one slash (e.g. "a/b/c")

`validateQualifiedName` in `pkg/apis/resource/validation/validation.go` and its declarative counterpart `resourcesQualifiedName` in `staging/src/k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/api/validate/strfmt.go` both split a qualified name on / and only handle `len(parts) == 1` and `len(parts) == 2` in their `switch` statements. A name with more than one slash (e.g. `"a/b/c"`) matches neither case, so no validation error is returned — the value is implicitly accepted.

Both functions already carry a `TODO` acknowledging this gap and explicitly cross-referencing each other:

```
// TODO: This validation is incomplete. It should reject qualified names
// that contain more than one slash. Currently, names like "a/b/c" are not
// handled and are implicitly accepted.
//
// This needs to be fixed in two places:
// 1. Here in this function.
// 2. In the corresponding declarative validation utility `resourcesQualifiedName`
//    in `staging/src/k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/api/validate/strfmt.go`.
//
// The fix should be introduced carefully, possibly using ratcheting to avoid
[... 1 more lines]
```

A qualified name with more than one slash should be rejected by both validation paths, consistent with how other malformed cases (empty prefix, empty name) are already handled in each function.

**How can we reproduce it (as minimally and precisely as possible)?**

1. Construct a DRA-related object (e.g. a `Device's` attribute or capacity key, which uses `resource.QualifiedName`) with a qualified name containing two or more slashes, such as `"a/b/c"`.
2. Run it through either `validateQualifiedName` or `resourcesQualifiedName`.
3. Observe: no validation error is returned for either path.

Verified via static code reading against current `master` (both functions read directly, TODO comments confirmed present and unresolved in both). Not yet reproduced against a running cluster/apiserver — happy to add unit test coverage for both functions alongside a fix, if a fix along these lines is the preferred direction. Note the TODO's own caution about "ratcheting" — since both call sites validate live, persisted API object fields (unlike a one-off config-validation case), a straightforward `default`: rejection could affect already-existing objects with malformed names; happy to discuss the right mitigation (e.g. ratcheting via `op.Type`, given the declarative validation system already threads `operation.Operation` through `resourcesQualifiedName`).

apiserver_init_events_total counts events after watcher was already terminated

`cacheWatcher.processInterval` keeps iterating the replay initial events even after the watcher has alredy been terminated, which cause the counter is over-report those event that never send to client.

I'm not sure if current behavior is bydesigned or a valid bug?

IMHO, `apiserver_init_events_total` should be the exactly number that send initial events to client.

Running a stress test to kube-apiserver:
1. launch multi processors to create and keep update objects;
2. launch multi processors which has an informer to the this object resource;
3. be able to see "short watch" log in client side and apiserver side;
4. In this monment, `apiserver_init_events_total` can increase insanely and not match with`apiserver_watch_events_total`. In my test env, it can be 1.5M/s.

